export const SKILLS_DATA: Record<string, string> = {"dance-science":"# SKILL: the science of what makes a human dance (and how the studio applies it)\n\nGroove is the measurable urge to move. The research below is what the numbers in MEASURED SOUND are for. Every rule ends with the studio move that follows from it.\n\n## What the research says (sources at the end)\n1. Syncopation follows an inverted U. Medium syncopation in the drums produces the most wanting-to-move and the most pleasure; none is boring, too much breaks the beat (Witek et al. 2014). MOVE: keep the kick straight and put the syncopation in hats, perc, ghost snares and the bass offbeats; ghost hits at chance 0.5-0.8 give medium complexity without a busy grid.\n2. Low frequencies carry the beat. People time their movement to the bass, and the auditory system times low pitches more precisely than high ones (Hove et al. 2014). Low-frequency spectral flux predicts head and hip movement (Burger et al. 2013); a louder kick drum makes people move more (Van Dyck et al. 2013); very-low-frequency bass people cannot consciously hear still raised dancing by about 12 percent (Cameron et al. 2022). MOVE: kick every beat at velocity 120 or more, sub on the roots, drop sub+low share 0.55-0.8, sidechain pump 0.3-0.6 so the kick owns its moment.\n3. Tempo has a body. Preferred movement tempo sits around 2 Hz, which is 120 BPM, the tempo of a brisk walk (MacDougall and Moore 2005); groove ratings and spontaneous movement peak in the 100-130 BPM band and stay high to about 140 (Janata et al. 2012; Etani et al. 2018). MOVE: house lives 122-130, tech-house 124-128, hard-house and speed garage 138-145 work because the half-time feel (claps and stabs on 2 and 4 at 71 BPM) keeps the body at walking pace.\n4. Microtiming is not the magic. Systematic millisecond offsets do not raise groove ratings and often lower them; quantized versions were rated as high or higher (Senn et al. 2016; Frühauf et al. 2013; Davies et al. 2013). What listeners call swing comes from accent patterns, event density and where the offbeats sit, not from random lateness. MOVE: shape velocities (accents, ladders), use the Groove Pool templates at moderate amounts, never random jitter above a few milliseconds; a clap 10-20 ms late is a pocket, 60 ms late is a mistake.\n5. Expectation is the engine. Pleasure comes from predictions that are confirmed with a twist (Huron 2006). Repetition builds the prediction; the variation every 8 bars pays it off. MOVE: every 8 bars something mutates (variant, automation, an element in or out); the hook repeats at bar 1 and bar 5 of the drop.\n6. The drop works because of the build. In EDM the breakdown-build-drop shape produces the largest peaks of movement and pleasure, and the size of the drop's effect scales with the tension before it (Solberg and Jensenius 2017, 2019). MOVE: breakdowns lose the kick and drop under 0.45 energy, builds end in a cut with a riser and a half beat of silence, drop 2 is denser or louder than drop 1.\n7. Loud bass is felt as well as heard. Above roughly 90 dB the vestibular system responds to bass and adds to the sense of motion (Todd and Cody 2000). MOVE: the club master targets -10 to -7 LUFS with a clean sub; a crushed master (above -6 LUFS) removes the very dynamics the pump depends on.\n8. Moving together feels good. Synchrony with others raises affiliation and pleasure (Hove and Risen 2009). MOVE: the four-on-the-floor kick and a clear downbeat every bar are the shared clock; never obscure bar one.\n\n## The dance checklist the studio scores against\n- Kick density 4 per bar in house lanes (halved in broken lanes), velocity 120 or more.\n- sub+low share 0.55-0.8 in the drop; mids present (lowMid+mid 0.12-0.3) so the record is not only weight.\n- Medium syncopation: offbeat hats, ghost notes with chance, bass on the offbeat eighths; kick and clap straight.\n- Pump 0.3-0.6 (tech-house) or 0.4-0.7 (hard-house).\n- Contrast: breakdown energy under 0.45, drop 1.0, drop 2 above drop 1.\n- Mutation every 8 bars; hook repetition at bars 1 and 5.\n- Master -10 to -7 LUFS, peak at or under 0 dBFS, never crushed.\n\n## Sources (verify page numbers before quoting in print)\nWitek, Clarke, Wallentin, Kringelbach, Vuust (2014) PLoS ONE 9(4) e94446 · Hove, Marie, Bruce, Trainor (2014) PNAS 111(28) · Burger, Thompson, Luck, Saarikallio, Toiviainen (2013) Frontiers in Psychology 4:183 · Van Dyck et al. (2013) Music Perception 30(4) · Cameron, Dotov, Flaten, Bosnyak, Hove, Trainor (2022) Current Biology 32(21) · MacDougall and Moore (2005) J Appl Physiol 99 · Janata, Tomic, Haberman (2012) JEP: General 141(1) · Etani, Marui, Kawase, Keller (2018) Frontiers in Psychology 9:462 · Senn, Kilchenmann, von Georgi, Bullerjahn (2016) Frontiers in Psychology 7:1487 · Frühauf, Kopiez, Platz (2013) Musicae Scientiae 17(2) · Davies, Madison, Silva, Gouyon (2013) Music Perception 30(5) · Huron (2006) Sweet Anticipation, MIT Press · Solberg and Jensenius (2017) Empirical Musicology Review 12(1-2); (2019) Music Perception 36(3) · Todd and Cody (2000) J Acoust Soc Am 107(1) · Hove and Risen (2009) Social Cognition 27(6).","genres":"# SKILL: every genre has a DNA (quick reference; the ten studio lanes carry full recipes in LANE RECIPE)\n\nUse this when the user names a genre outside the ten lanes or asks how a lane differs. Numbers are typical centers, not laws.\n\n| Genre | Tempo | Kick | Bass | Swing and feel | Key habits | The tell |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| House | 120-128 | 4 on the floor | offbeat 8ths or walking | swung 16th hats 0.54-0.58 | minor, some major | piano or organ stabs, disco chops |\n| Tech-house | 124-128 | 4 on the floor, tight | rolling offbeat saw | 0.52-0.56 | minor | the bass IS the hook, sparse tops |\n| Deep house | 118-124 | 4 on the floor, soft | sub with a 7th color | 0.55-0.6 | minor 7ths, jazzy | warm chords, long pads |\n| Melodic house and techno | 120-126 | 4 on the floor | sub root, arps above | near straight | minor, modal | arpeggios, long builds, emotional breaks |\n| Afro house | 118-124 | 4 with syncopated toms | tonal percussive bass | 0.56-0.62 | minor pentatonic | log drums, shakers, call and response |\n| Techno | 128-140 | pounding 4, distorted | drone or 16th pulse | straight, 909 | minor or none | rumble kick, hypnotic loops, no drop |\n| Hard-house and speed garage | 138-145 | saturated 4 | warp or reese lead bass | skippy ghost snares | flat minors (F, Eb, Bb) | hoover stabs, diva chops, open hat every offbeat 8th |\n| UK garage and 2-step | 130-138 | 2-step, kick skips beat 3 | sub with slides | strong 0.6+ shuffle | minor | swung snares, chopped vocals |\n| Jersey club | 130-140 | the five-kick pattern | 808 sub | straight | any | bed squeak, triplet kicks, chants |\n| Trance | 136-142 | 4 on the floor | offbeat rolling bass | straight | minor, uplifting | supersaw leads, 32-bar builds |\n| Drum and bass | 170-176 | 2-step breakbeat | reese or sub | straight, dense | minor | fast breaks over half-time bass |\n| Dubstep | 140 (70 half-time) | sparse, on 1 | wobble or growl | straight | minor | half-time drop, LFO bass |\n| Trap and club rap | 140-160 (70-80) | 808 kick as bass | 808 slides | triplet hats | minor | rolling hats, 808 glides |\n| Afrobeats | 100-112 | syncopated | round sub | laid back | major and minor | log drum, guitar licks |\n| Reggaeton | 88-98 | dembow | sub on the dembow | straight | minor | the dembow tresillo |\n| Disco and nu-disco | 110-122 | 4 on the floor | octave bass | slight swing | major, minor 7ths | strings, guitar, claps |\n| Pop | 95-125 | varies | root and fifth | straight | major, relative minor | chorus lift, four-chord loops |\n| Lo-fi | 70-90 | swung, dusty | mellow bass | 0.6+ | major 7ths | vinyl crackle, jazz chords |\n\nRules: a genre named outside the ten lanes still needs a lane id for the engine; pick the nearest lane (\"techno\" for dubstep or drum and bass drums, \"trap-club\" for trap, \"afro-house\" for afrobeats, \"deep-house\" for disco and lo-fi) and adjust tempo and feel with ops. Say which lane you chose and why in one sentence.","sampling-and-stems":"# SKILL: sampling, stems and one-shots from a real record\n\nWhen the user brings a record (an upload or a link), the studio can separate it into stems (drums, bass, vocals, other), measure it, and slice drum one-shots. Here is how a producer uses that material and where the lines are.\n\n## What the studio gives you\n- STEMS: drums, bass, vocals, other, each as an audio clip the ears have measured (bpm, key).\n- ONE-SHOTS: kick, snare or clap, hat sliced from the drums stem, each under a second, trimmed to the transient with a short fade. They arrive as library clips usable with role \"audio\" and clip.oneShot true.\n- FACTS: the record's tempo, key, structure, best 8 bars, groove map, band shares.\n\n## How to use a drum one-shot like a producer\n- Kick: put it on the kick grid (\"X...X...X...X...\"), midi 60 = as recorded. Tune it so the fundamental lands on the root or the fifth of the song key (clip.semitones, stay within plus or minus 4). Layer the synth kick's click on top if the sample is soft; highpass everything else under 30 Hz.\n- Hat or shaker: run it through the song groove; velocity ladder X.g.x.g. ; a little chance (0.6-0.8) on the ghosts.\n- Clap or snare: on 2 and 4, 10-20 ms late pocket (clapLateMs), reverb send 0.1-0.2.\n- Never loop the whole drums stem under your own drums; take the sounds, keep your grid.\n\n## How to use a melodic stem or a vocal\n- Pitch it to the song key first (clip.semitones), warp it to the tempo (clip.warp, clip.bpm from the facts).\n- Vocals: highpass 150-200 Hz, an 8n delay, a phrase at bar 1 of the drop and its echo at bar 5; chops on the offbeats for garage and hard-house. A sung phrase pitched to the fifth or up an octave becomes a diva chop.\n- \"Other\" stem (chords, pads): use it only as a reference for the progression; write your own chords.\n\n## The lines (say these plainly if asked)\n- The user's uploads and links are analyzed for their own projects. The studio keeps the stems in their account only; it never redistributes them.\n- A recording that is not theirs cannot be released without clearance from the rights holders, even chopped. Say so once, without a lecture, and offer the legal equivalent: the DNA (tempo, key, arc, groove), your own notes, and the CC0 library sounds.\n- Never call a rebuilt part \"the original\". Emulation is the craft here.","song-breakdown":"# SKILL: breaking a record down and finding its best pieces\n\nYou break a song down the way a producer does in a listening session: structure first, then energy, then the one thing that makes it that record. You reason from MEASURED SOUND (the ears), from STEMS when the studio has separated them, and from the DNA of the lane. You never copy a recording; you learn its DNA and write your own notes.\n\n## The breakdown, in order (say it in this order, one breath each)\n1. Frame: tempo, key or its relative, lane, and how long until the first drop (the collection median is about 30 seconds, 15 percent into the record).\n2. Structure map: the sections with their energy (per-bar energy 0-1 in the facts), where the kick leaves, where the drop lands, how long each block is. Name the shape (intro 16, build 8, drop 16, break 16, drop 32 is a common club shape).\n3. The engine: which element carries the identity. In tech-house it is almost always the bass hook; in hard-house the warp bass plus the stab; in melodic house the chord progression; in garage the swung drums and the chop. Say which and why (band shares, what repeats most).\n4. The hook: the phrase that repeats at bar 1 and bar 5 of the drop. Note its register, length (one or two bars), rhythm (offbeat or downbeat), and whether it answers the bass.\n5. The drum language: kicks per bar, where the clap sits, open-hat placement, ghost notes, swing (facts.groove.swing and the groove map), fills before transitions.\n6. The mix picture: sub+low share, mids, air, pump, loudness, width. Compare to the lane DNA.\n7. The trick: the one production move that makes the record memorable (a filter that opens for 16 bars, a vocal chop pitched to the fifth, a silence before the drop, a half-time bar). Name it and how to do it here.\n\n## Finding the best pieces (what to take, never the recording itself)\n- BEST 8 BARS: the window with the highest mean energy that also contains the most repeated melodic motif; usually bars 1-8 of the first drop. Say the time as m:ss.\n- THE DRUM SOUND: from the drums stem, the kick with the cleanest low fundamental (40-60 Hz), the hat with the most consistent transient, the clap with the widest stereo image. The studio can slice these as one-shots; you pitch the kick so its fundamental sits on the root or fifth of the new key.\n- THE GROOVE MAP: the measured 16-slot timing of the hats (facts.groove.grooveMap). Borrow it with {\"op\":\"groove\",\"track\":\"all\",\"name\":\"custom\",\"from\":\"reference\"}.\n- THE BASS IDEA: the rhythm pattern of the bass (which offbeats), not its notes. Write your own notes in the new key.\n- THE ARC: section lengths, energy contour, drop position. This is DNA and it is free to emulate.\n\n## Turning a breakdown into moves\n- Reference loaded: close the biggest delta first (tempo, then key, then sub+low, then pump, then structure). Say which delta you closed.\n- Emulating a named artist: use ARTIST DNA medians for tempo, key family, loudness and band shares; write the hook in that artist's rhythm language, never their melody.\n- \"Make it like this song\" is a rollout (action emulate) unless one element is named; then it is ops.\n- Never claim a part is \"the same as\" the record. Say \"in the spirit of\" and name the DNA you borrowed.","vocals":"# SKILL: vocals in a dance record\n\n## What a vocal does in these lanes\n- Tech-house: one spoken or sung phrase, dry and close, on the downbeat of the drop and again at bar 5; often a single word chopped on the offbeat.\n- Hard-house and speed garage: pitched diva chops, big reverb tail in the breakdown, dry stabs of the same syllable in the drop; the chop sits a fifth or an octave above the sung note.\n- Melodic house and trance: a full sung hook in the breakdown, thinned to one line in the drop so the lead can breathe.\n- Afro house: call and response, layered low harmonies, percussive syllables.\n\n## How the studio does it today\n- Library vocals (SAMPLES AVAILABLE) as one-shots: midi 60 as recorded, 67 a fifth up, 72 an octave up. Tune toward the key with clip.semitones within plus or minus 4 so the formant stays human.\n- An uploaded sung phrase: warp to tempo, highpass 150-200 Hz, an 8n delay at wet 0.2-0.3, reverb send 0.2-0.35 in breakdowns and 0.1 in drops.\n- Hum to melody: action recordHum for the topline; then a vocal-like lead (pluck or lead with long release) carries it until a singer or a vocal render replaces it.\n- Placement law: the vocal owns bar 1 of the drop; the bass owns bars 2-4. Vocal and lead never speak at the same time.\n\n## Writing a topline that lands\n- One idea, six to ten syllables, vowels open (ah, oh, ay), repeated at bar 1 and bar 5, varied once at bar 13.\n- Rhythm first: write the syllables on the grid as notes, then the pitch. Offbeat entries in garage lanes, downbeat entries in tech-house.\n- Range: a fifth to an octave; the hook note is the root or the fifth; the color note is the b3 or b7.\n- A vocal render layer (full sung vocals from a model) is a future studio feature. Until it lands, say so and use the tools above."};
